BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Playing Masquerade: Parading Radical Participation
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260605T130000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260605T170000
UID:playing-masquerade-parading-radical-participation@ocadu.ca
DESCRIPTION:As part of this year’s inaugural Toronto Climate Week (TOCW)
 \, the GCCA is hosting its two-part closing event for 2025/26 academic yea
 r. Come join us celebrate the start of summer and the work of our very own
  BMO Sustainable Futures Faculty Fellows\, Assistant Professor Michael Lee
  Poy along with fashion and textile Assistant Professor Leesa Hamilton fro
 m NSCAD University and Toronto Metropolitan University graduate student Mo
 nica Polo of mpolo designs inc.\n\nThe Global Centre for Climate Action BM
 O Climate Action Speaker Series This event offers multiple portions\; plea
 se REGISTER for each portion individually: - Presentations 1:30 PM - 3:00 
 PM - Workshop + Parade 3:00 PM - 4:30 PM Participants are welcome to atten
 d either portion independently\, though attending both is encouraged as th
 e talks directly inform the collaborative workshop and parade experience. 
 Join us! As part of this year’s inaugural Toronto Climate Week (TOCW) \,
  the GCCA is hosting its two-part closing event for 2025/26 academic year.
  Come join us celebrate the start of summer and the work of our very own B
 MO Sustainable Futures Faculty Fellows\, Assistant Professor Michael Lee P
 oy along with fashion and textile Assistant Professor Leesa Hamilton from 
 NSCAD University and Toronto Metropolitan University graduate student Moni
 ca Polo of mpolo designs inc. Generously supported by BMO.\n\nPRESENTATION
 S: What if climate action felt like a parade: creative\, collective\, and 
 impossible to ignore? Well\, it can! Playing Masquerade is a climate gathe
 ring that transforms conversation into costume and collective anxiety into
  collective action. Part presentation\, part workshop\, part parade – we
  invite participants to imagine new forms of climate intervention through 
 art\, movement\, and radical participation.\n\nWORKSHOP + PARADE: Rooted i
 n the ecologies surrounding OCAD University’s campus\, we are drawing in
 spiration from OCAD University’s pollinator garden\, Toronto’s native 
 pollinators\, and migratory species to reimagine public spaces and partici
 pation as sites of collective gathering and climate imagination. After the
  series of presentations exploring participatory design\, collective expre
 ssion\, and creative climate interventions\, we invite participants to bec
 ome part of the experience themselves. We will then transition into a coll
 aborative costume-making workshop using reused\, recycled\, and upcycled m
 aterials to create masks\, costumes\, and wearable interventions for the p
 arade to follow. The event culminates in a celebratory sidewalk parade aro
 und the OCAD University campus accompanied by music – reclaiming public 
 space for visibility\, climate imagination\, and radical public participat
 ion. Open to everyone and all ages. No prior experience necessary. Partici
 pants are encouraged to bring hats\, reusable materials\, and empty plasti
 c bottles or containers that can be incorporated into their costumes\, dec
 orated during the workshop\, or transformed into handheld noisemakers for 
 the parade. As the TDSB has an elementary school PA day planned for that d
 ay\, please feel free to register with your family. Please note that works
 hop capacity is limited. The workshop portion will conclude with a celebra
 tory parade around the OCAD University campus - attendees are encouraged t
 o stay and join the parade\, even if they are unable to register for the w
 orkshop. Come join us! Come participate! Come play masquerade!\n\nMichael 
 Lee Poy OCAD University Michael Lee Poy is an Afro-Caribbean artist-activi
 st and architect from Trinidad and Tobago and Canada. His practice and int
 erests are centered on post-colonial Caribbean design and fabrication in t
 he festival arts – specifically Carnival. He is currently developing ped
 agogy to address the absence of masquerade design\, construction\, and pre
 sentation in curriculum through the Carnival Architectonics course. Michae
 l has been incubating Moko Jumbie (stilt dancer) Mascamp workshops in Trin
 idad\, Cleveland and Toronto for the last decade. Similar to a studio lear
 ning ecosystem\, mascamps are socially conscious design build fabrication 
 laboratories for innovation. The Moko Jumbie Mascamp focuses on the sustai
 nable design and fabrication of costumes (regalia) in a co-creative and sa
 fe environment.\n\nLeesa Hamilton NSCAD University Leesa Hamilton is an As
 sistant Professor in Textiles/Fashion with NSCAD University. She was born 
 and raised in Kjipuktuk/Halifax and is of Filipino-Canadian ancestry. Lees
 a is a costume designer\, textile artist\, community arts facilitator and 
 consultant. Through her practice and research\, she explores textiles\, co
 stume and fashion as tools for community building\, storytelling and socia
 l change. Leesa studied costume with Dalhousie University\, Fashion with G
 eorge Brown College and holds a Master of Arts in Education from OISE/Univ
 ersity of Toronto. Leesa’s current research falls under NSCAD’s Sow to
  Sew and Art Factory Programming where she works with students and communi
 ty to co-develop community-based programming that explore identity\, repre
 sentation and allyship through textiles and garment making. Programs take 
 the form of residencies\, mentorships\, free schools and camps.\n\nMonica 
 Polo Toronto Metropolitan University / mpolo designs inc. Monica Polo is a
  leading interior design professional specializing in sustainable\, health
 -focused environments. With over 30 years of expertise in design and educa
 tion\, she pioneers methodologies that promote wellbeing and sustainabilit
 y through creative art and play. Currently completing her Master’s in En
 vironmental Applied Science and Management at TMU\, she founded Ecoapt –
  A Sustainable Living Lab. This immersive space invites guests to explore 
 eco- friendly alternatives and overcome the "inertia bump" of starting the
 ir sustainable journey. Leading by example\, Monica has achieved a Net Zer
 o home\, showcasing urban sustainability retrofits and empowering homeowne
 rs. Through mpolo designs inc.\, she collaborates with institutions like S
 unnybrook Health Centre’s Green Task Force to drive strategic sustainabi
 lity initiatives\, including educational artwork. She also mentors the nex
 t generation\, believing the journey toward sustainable choices is as impo
 rtant as the destination.
LOCATION:Main Building & Rosalie Sharp Centre for Design (MCA) 100 McCaul 
 Street Presentations: MCA 190 Auditorium Workshop + Parade: Butterfield Pa
 rk
URL:https://www.ocadu.ca/events-and-exhibitions/playing-masquerade-paradin
 g-radical-participation
X-SOURCE:OCAD University
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:How Technology Rewrote the Self\, One Click at a Time
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260602T170000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260602T190000
UID:how-technology-rewrote-self-one-click-time@ocadu.ca
DESCRIPTION:Join celebrated innovation strategist and author Alexander Man
 u for the launch of his provocative new book\, You Were Never Just Using I
 t: How Technology Rewrote the Self\, One Click at a Time.\n\nYOU WERE NEVE
 R JUST USING IT: How Technology Rewrote the Self\, One Click at a Time Boo
 k Launch & Conversation with Alexander Manu and Sinead Bovell 📍 OCAD Un
 iversity Auditorium\, Toronto 📅 Tuesday\, June 2\, 2026 🕔 5:00 PM 
 – 7:00 PM Join celebrated innovation strategist and author Alexander Man
 u for the launch of his provocative new book\, You Were Never Just Using I
 t: How Technology Rewrote the Self\, One Click at a Time. Manu will be in 
 conversation with Sinead Bovell — futurist\, entrepreneur\, and advocate
  for technology education and ethics\, widely recognized for her work brid
 ging the gap between emerging technologies and underrepresented communitie
 s. Dubbed “The AI Educator for Non-Nerds” by Vogue \, Bovell brings a 
 sharp and accessible lens to one of the defining questions of our time: Wh
 at if technology isn’t just changing how we live — but who we become? 
 In You Were Never Just Using It \, Manu argues that the real impact of tec
 hnology has never been the devices themselves\, but the subtle behavioural
  shifts they create. From desktops to smartphones to generative AI\, each 
 innovation quietly reshapes our habits\, identities\, relationships\, and 
 even our understanding of ourselves. Thought-provoking and deeply relevant
 \, this conversation will explore: How technology rewires human behaviour 
 The rise of AI and the “post-digital self” The hidden psychological ar
 chitecture of modern life What it means to remain human in an increasingly
  automated world Whether you work in technology\, design\, media\, educati
 on\, business\, or simply feel overwhelmed by the pace of change\, this ev
 ent offers a rare opportunity to step back and examine how digital life ha
 s transformed us. Following the discussion\, guests are invited to stay fo
 r a book signing. Books will be available for purchase on site. Presented 
 by OCAD University and Sutherland House Books.
LOCATION:Main Building & Rosalie Sharp Centre for Design (MCA) 100 McCaul 
 Street Auditorium\, Room 190
URL:https://www.ocadu.ca/events-and-exhibitions/how-technology-rewrote-sel
 f-one-click-time
X-SOURCE:OCAD University
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:OCAD U Gala
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260527T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260527T235900
UID:ocad-u-gala@ocadu.ca
DESCRIPTION:Convening Toronto's most creative and influential minds in the
  art and design sector\, the OCAD U Gala returns this spring!\n\nGET READY
  TO EXPERIENCE TORONTO’S FAST-GROWING ART AND DESIGN EVENT Celebrating t
 he talent and creativity of artists and designers in support of students a
 t OCAD University.\n\nFollowing a standout debut last spring\, the OCAD U 
 Gala returns on May 27\, 2026 \, as a rising highlight in Toronto’s cult
 ural calendar. This annual event brings together the city’s most creativ
 e thinkers for an immersive evening that showcases visionary ideas\, excep
 tional talent and the vibrant spirit of our University. With the support o
 f our generous sponsors\, guests will experience striking art activations\
 , bold design moments and an atmosphere alive with energy—reflecting the
  gala’s growing momentum as one of Toronto’s premier art and design ce
 lebrations.\n\nThe Event at a Glance VIP Reception Art Installations and E
 xperiences Dinner and Program Live Art Auction After Party For one night o
 nly\, guests will have the opportunity to bid on one-of-a-kind artwork by 
 an inspiring mix of emerging talent and established artists. All OCAD U al
 umni. Featuring: Pardiss Amerian\, Wendy Coburn\, Mitchell Fenton\, Camill
 e Jodoin-Eng\, Alexa Kumiko Hatanaka\, Megan McCabe\, Jordin Mimran\, Kris
 tine Moran\, Kristín Morthens\, Kyle Scheurmann\, John Scott\, Rosalie Sh
 arp\, Pixie Shaw\, Bewabon Shilling\, and Kendra Yee. Don’t miss your ch
 ance to be part of this exclusive event in the heart of Toronto’s vibran
 t art and design scene.\n\n2026 Art Auction Catalogue\n\nInside the Collec
 tion\n\nAdvancing Student Success Funds raised at the gala will establish 
 vital student bursaries at OCAD U that reduce financial barriers to a lead
 ing art and design university education. This crucial support fosters the 
 growth and talent of future creative leaders and innovators.\n\nBuy Gala T
 ickets\n\nThe celebration continues at the OCAD U Gala After Party! Experi
 ence the energy of the OCAD U community alongside Toronto’s most creativ
 e and influential minds in a vibrant\, immersive atmosphere filled with mu
 sic\, art\, cocktails and gourmet desserts. Tickets are available for purc
 hase now. Spots are limited—get yours before they’re gone! Dress Code:
  Creative Cocktail *Note: If you’ve already purchased a ticket or are at
 tending the OCAD U Gala\, your entry to the After Party is free.\n\nBuy Af
 ter Party Tickets\n\nThe OCAD U Gala Committee\n\nWe are grateful to our d
 edicated Gala Committee members\, whose vision and leadership are helping 
 bring this extraordinary event to life.\n\nAmy Burstyn-Fritz\, Co-Founder 
 of Misette - Co-Chair\n\nJeff Hull\, President\, Hullmark - Co-Chair\n\nCo
 lleen Baldwin Chris Braden Jaclyn Cobourn Alison Currie Rana Florida Alan 
 Gertner Nandini Gupta Alison Milne Jordin Mimran Tanya Taylor\n\nHighlight
 s from the Inaugural OCAD U Gala\n\nTHANK YOU TO OUR OCAD U GALA SPONSORS
LOCATION:Main Building & Rosalie Sharp Centre for Design (MCA) 100 McCaul 
 Street
URL:https://can01.safelinks.protection.outlook.com/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fsecu
 re3.convio.net%2Focadu%2Fsite%2FTicketing%3Fview%3DTickets%26id%3D102173&d
 ata=05%7C02%7Cwebsupport%40ocadu.ca%7C465295de16a74bff2eff08de845602e2%7C0
 6e469d12d2a468fae9b7df0968eb6d7%7C0%7C0%7C639093702745936498%7CUnknown%7CT
 WFpbGZsb3d8eyJFbXB0eU1hcGkiOnRydWUsIlYiOiIwLjAuMDAwMCIsIlAiOiJXaW4zMiIsIkF
 OIjoiTWFpbCIsIldUIjoyfQ%3D%3D%7C0%7C%7C%7C&sdata=lWAztG2H92Ve3uK4oLKZcN%2B
 VyIfZfwlZSqCVPycCY%2Fw%3D&reserved=0
